      "text": "We start by examining the quadratic equation whose roots are $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n+1}$:\n\\[x^2 - a_n x + a_{n-1} a_{n+1} = 0,\\]which has roots $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n+1}.$  Using Vieta's formulas, we have\n\\begin{align*}\na_{n-1} + a_{n+1} &= a_n, \\\\\na_{n-1}a_{n+1} &= a_n^2.\n\\end{align*}We also know that if $a_n = 1 + \\sqrt{7},$ then\n\\[a_{n-1} + a_{n+1} = a_n = 1 + \\sqrt{7},\\]and\n\\[a_{n-1} a_{n+1} = a_n^2 = (1 + \\sqrt{7})^2 = 8 + 2\\sqrt{7}.\\]If $a_n = 1 - \\sqrt{7},$ then\n\\[a_{n-1} + a_{n+1} = a_n = 1 - \\sqrt{7},\\]and\n\\[a_{n-1} a_{n+1} = a_n^2 = (1 - \\sqrt{7})^2 = 8 - 2\\sqrt{7}.\\]Therefore, when $a_n = 1 + \\sqrt{7},$ we have\n\\[\\frac{a_{n-1} + a_{n+1}}{a_{n-1} a_{n+1}} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{7}}{8 + 2\\sqrt{7}} = \\frac{(1 + \\sqrt{7})(8 - 2\\sqrt{7})}{(8 + 2\\sqrt{7})(8 - 2\\sqrt{7})} = \\frac 64 = \\frac 32,\\]so $a_{n-1} + a_{n+1} = (1 + \\sqrt{7})a_n = (1 + \\sqrt{7})^3 = 8 + 12\\sqrt{7}.$  Similarly, we have\n\\[\\frac{a_{n-1} + a_{n+1}}{a_{n-1} a_{n+1}} = \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{7}}{8 - 2\\sqrt{7}} = \\frac{(1 - \\sqrt{7})(8 + 2\\sqrt{7})}{(8 - 2\\sqrt{7})(8 + 2\\sqrt{7})} = \\frac 64 = \\frac 32,\\]so $a_{n-1} + a_{n+1} = (1 - \\sqrt{7})a_n = (1 - \\sqrt{7})^3 = 8 - 12\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, $a_{1776} = (1 + \\sqrt{7})^3 = 8 + 12\\sqrt{7} = (1 + \\sqrt{7})(1 + 11\\sqrt{7}).$  The answer is a multiple of $(1 + \\sqrt{7}),$ so it is $(1 + \\sqrt{7})^3$ or $13 + \\sqrt{7},$ so the answer is $\\boxed{13}.$. The answer is 13",
